The greatest possible value of \(\gcd(n+7, 2n+1)\) is found by noting that any common divisor \(d\) must also divide the linear combination \((2n+1) - 2(n+7) = -13\). Thus \(d\) divides 13, so \(d\) can be at most 13. 

To see if 13 is attainable, set \(n \equiv 6 \pmod{13}\). For example, \(n = 6\) gives \(n+7 = 13\) and \(2n+1 = 13\), so \(\gcd = 13\).

\boxed{13}

The number of integers from 1 to 100 that are divisible by 2 is \( \left\lfloor \frac{100}{2} \right\rfloor = 50 \). Among these, those divisible by both 2 and 3 are divisible by 6. The number of multiples of 6 in this range is \( \left\lfloor \frac{100}{6} \right\rfloor = 16 \). Therefore, the number of integers divisible by 2 and not by 3 is \( 50 - 16 = 34 \).

The probability is \( \frac{34}{100} = \frac{17}{50} \).

\boxed{\frac{17}{50}}

The measure of \(\angle APC\) is \(74^\circ\).

We can verify this by placing \(A\) at the origin, \(B\) at \((1,0)\), and \(C\) at \((\cos 92^\circ, \sin 92^\circ)\) (since \(AB = AC = 1\)). Let \(P\) and \(Q\) lie on rays from \(A\) at \(20^\circ\) and \(66^\circ\) respectively. Setting \(BP = PQ = QC\) and solving the resulting trigonometric system shows that the configuration forces \(\angle APC = 74^\circ\). In particular, the law of sines in \(\triangle APC\) yields \(p = AP = \frac{\sin 34^\circ}{\sin 74^\circ}\) and \(PC = \frac{\sin 72^\circ}{\sin 74^\circ}\), and these satisfy the equal-length conditions exactly.

\boxed{74}
